## Correctness the first release must not ship

### The demo chat routes deny by default

`src/transport/routes/chats.ts:33` falls back to a pass-through middleware when no HTTP authentication is configured, so a consumer who configures only WebSocket auth publishes an unauthenticated chat API beside an authenticated socket. An unauthenticated caller can drive up to `maxToolRounds` model calls per request, billed and forwarded to the model vendor. The engine's own `example/server.ts` ships this configuration.

- The fallback becomes a middleware that answers `401`, for all three routes.
- `createChatRoutes`, `createApp` and `HalEngineConfig` keep their signatures. This is a default change, not a type change.
- A consumer terminating authentication at a gateway writes the pass-through themselves, one line, in their own repository.
- `specs/hal-engine-chat-routes/spec.md` § When the guard does nothing currently describes the old default as behaviour and is corrected here.

### The ownership guard runs

`src/transport/routes/chats.ts:35` records `req.user?.id ?? 'anonymous'` as the chat's owner, and `authorizedChat` compares owners only when the caller's id is truthy. A consumer who authenticates correctly but attaches the user somewhere other than `req.user` gets every chat owned by the string `'anonymous'` and readable by everyone, with no error anywhere.

- The `'anonymous'` fallback is removed.
- `AuthenticatedRequest` — `Request` plus optional `user` — is exported from the types layer and re-exported from the public surface, and `HttpAuthMiddleware` is restated in its terms, so the contract for what a middleware must produce is written down.
- The four ad-hoc inline casts in `chats.ts` are replaced by that exported type.
- A guard between the auth middleware and each handler answers `401` when `req.user` is absent or its `id` is `undefined`, `null` or `''`. The empty string is named explicitly because `id` is `string | number` and `0` is a legal id that a truthiness check would wrongly deny.

### The routes say what they are

`createChatRoutes` takes a `SessionStore` and ignores it — the parameter is named `_sessionStore` — and keeps every chat in a process-local `Map`. A chat id created over REST never reaches a WebSocket session: the socket path matcher never parses one, the connection handler mints its own session id, and inbound validation discards the field. The documented flow of creating a chat over REST and then connecting to it has never been joined.

- The export stays. It is public surface of a package about to be published, and the only worked example in the repository of lifting auth headers off an Express request into a `ChatSession`.
- The `_sessionStore` parameter stays. Dropping it is a breaking change for no gain.
- The file states, at its head, that the store is unused by design, that the `Map` is process-local, and that a REST-created chat id is not a WebSocket session id.

### The example is the only correct file, and nothing checked it

`tsconfig.json` sets `rootDir` to `src/` and excludes `example/`, so the one file the README and the guide are written against was never compiled. Both drifted from it: three `PromptBuilderConfig` fields under names the type does not have, a `ws` authenticator taking a token rather than the upgrade request and returning `userId` rather than `id`, an `engine.listen()` that does not exist, and a documented `orchestrator.hooks` that `createHalEngine` never forwards.

- `tsconfig.example.json` compiles `example/` against `src/` with `noEmit`, run as `typecheck:example` and called from CI.
- The README quick start and the guide's minimal setup are `example/server.ts` apart from the import specifier.
- `OrchestratorHooks` is documented where it actually lives, on `createChatOrchestrator`, not on `HalEngineConfig`.

### `onConnect` is called

`HalEngineConfig.onConnect` is declared at `src/config.ts:37`, forwarded at `:75`, and declared again on `HalServerOptions` at `src/transport/createServer.ts:20` — and then omitted from the `deps` object at `:50` that the connection handler receives. Its sibling `onDisconnect` is forwarded on the adjacent line and is invoked on every close, so a consumer sees half the pair work. Four committed documents say both work.
</document>
<document source="specs/hal-engine-architecture/spec.md" type="spec" relevance="0.99" tokens="1111">
## Tool Execution Loop

At the start of every turn, after the input hooks and before `beforeModelResponse`, the orchestrator reads the `ToolRegistry` once. That read gives it the definitions it sends the provider and the `promptInstructions` of each tool, which `PromptBuilder` assembles alongside the AI's identity, domain context, and response guidelines into the system prompt for that turn.

### What a turn reads from the registry

- A tool registered after the orchestrator was built is sent to the provider and named in the system prompt, under the default tool preamble, from the next turn, so a registry that starts empty may be filled after construction ([validated by: sends weather, registered after the orchestrator was built on an empty registry, as the only tool and names it under the default preamble](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L48)).
- A tool registered between two turns is absent from the first turn's request and present in the second's ([validated by: sends tools undefined on the first turn and weather alone on the second when weather is registered between them](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L65)).
- The read is per turn, not per round: a tool registered while a turn runs, by one of its executors say, reaches no later round of that turn, so the list the model sees does not change while an unanswered `tool_use` block from an earlier round is still in its messages ([validated by: keeps weather, registered by an executor mid-turn, out of round 2 of that turn and sends it from the next turn](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L77)).
- A registry with no tools sends the provider `tools: undefined`, never an empty array, because Bedrock and Vertex read an empty list differently; Bedrock's request then carries no `toolConfig` key ([validated by: calls sendMessage with tools undefined, and Bedrock builds a request with no toolConfig key](../../src/emptyToolList.test.ts#L28)).
- A definition registered as a thunk is read once at the start of each turn for what that turn advertises, so a definition it changes between turns reaches the provider changed ([validated by: invokes a definition thunk once in each of two turns that call no tool, so the second turn sends its changed description](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L99)).
- Each call the turn executes reads the thunk once more, to validate that call's input, so a turn calling the tool once invokes it twice ([validated by: invokes a definition thunk twice in a turn that calls its tool once: at the turn start, then for the call](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L124)).
- A hook sees the same read: `beforeModelResponse` receives a prompt naming a tool registered after `createHalEngine` returned ([validated by: hands beforeModelResponse a prompt naming weather, a tool registered after createHalEngine returned](../../src/config.test.ts#L167)).
- Every tool `description`, `inputSchema` and `promptInstructions` string is sent to the model provider every turn, not once per process, so under GDPR a definition carries no personal data and no internal hostname; the orchestrator logs a tool's name and never its definition ([validated by: writes no tool description, input schema or prompt instructions to the log over a turn that calls the tool](../../src/orchestration/toolsPerTurn.test.ts#L142)).

Because the advertised list is re-read every turn, leaving a tool unregistered or unadvertised is not an access control, which matters for the human oversight Article 14 of the EU AI Act asks for. A policy that must keep a capability from being used enforces it at execution, in `beforeToolCall` or in the executor itself. The registry stays process-wide: there is no per-session or per-user tool list, and no `unregister`.

When the AI model decides it needs data (like weather information or a database lookup), it requests a tool call. The orchestrator handles this in a loop:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Send messages to AI Provider] --> B[Stream response chunks]
    B --> C{stopReason?}
    C -->|end_turn| D[Done]
    C -->|tool_use| E[Collect tool calls]
    E --> H{rounds executed < maxToolRounds?}
    H -->|yes| F[Execute tools in parallel via Promise.all]
    H -->|no: budget exhausted| X[Log tool budget exhausted and end the turn]
    F --> G[Add tool results to messages]
    G --> A
```

